Title: "Structural Declarations, Value Declarations, And Equality"
Id: "1aa3c3a3-9052-446a-905b-d2e6c8077afb"

Text: "MathLingua uses related but distinct operators:

- `::=` introduces structural shape
- `:=` introduces a value definition
- `=` is mathematical equality

Examples:

```mlg-fragment
G ::= (X, *, e)
S := \set.theoretic.pair:of{A}:and{A}
x = y
```

Only `::=` introduces symbols; the right-hand side of `:=` must already be in scope. A `Declares:` target often combines a value definition with a type: `S := \set.theoretic.pair:of{A}:and{A} is \set`.

A chained function declaration keeps the function form and names its output:

```mlg-fragment
X ::= x(i_) ::= y_
```

This introduces the alias `X`, the mapping name `x`, the input `i_`, and the output `y_`; `X` and `x` denote the same mapping. When the subject is a function declaration such as `f(x_, y_)`, the `::=` expansion must be a single placeholder output such as `z_`.

Combining `::=` with `:=` also creates a local syntactic substitution: if `A ::= B := B` is in scope, facts about `A` can satisfy requirements about `B` and vice versa, because the two keys normalize together.

A declaration may combine structural shape and value definition. In set expressions, `:=` value bindings belong in the predicate branch after `|`:

```mlg-fragment
{z_ ::= (a_, b_) : a_ \"in\" A; b_ \"in\" B | z_ := \ordered.pair:of{a_}:and{b_}}
```"
Id: "13138234-2a4e-4796-9f25-a8db2c6a38a2"
